-Use the provided spreadsheet tools to complete the requested workbook. Preserve source inputs.
+Spreadsheet work method (follow every step; do not skip the verification loop).
+
+1. Read before writing. Run workbook_inspect on the source and list every non-empty cell, its label, its value and its number format. Read every cell comment: comments carry binding business rules (which rate applies to which adjustment, which cells are intentional blanks, which period is "final"). Quote those rules in your plan before choosing formulas.
+
+2. Map semantics to a formula for every required cell. Build a small table: row label -> meaning -> formula pattern. Common conventions:
+   - Subtotals = SUM(range); differences = end - opening; ratios = numerator / denominator with the denominator row taken from the same column.
+   - Tax and rate adjustments apply only to the specific item they describe, never to the whole base.
+   - Sign and direction matter: e.g. removing an expense adds it back to profit; removing a gain subtracts it; a lower balance grows when a repayment is subtracted.
+   - Items computed "from opening principal", "from original amount" or "per year" use an absolute or original figure, not a prior-period closing balance.
+   - Scenario/case switches: use INDEX/MATCH, CHOOSE or IF on the case cell so the requested case selects the row set; never hard-code the chosen case's numbers.
+   - Only-final or only-last-period cells: compute them in that period only and leave other periods legitimately blank if the sheet is blank there.
+   - Units: currency, units and percent rows differ; keep each row's unit consistent with its label.
+
+3. Write formulas, never constants, into every required cell. Use Excel syntax with commas, quotes around text, and no leading "=" duplication. Make the first column's formulas correct, then use workbook_fill_row so the same relative pattern extends across the remaining columns — this keeps every period consistent. For non-contiguous cells, use workbook_write with one edit per cell.
+
+4. Verify numerically before delivering. Always pass verify=true on your final write (or re-run a write with verify=true). Read the returned computed values for every edited cell and check each against an independent hand calculation you computed yourself (with bash/python3 if useful) from the source inputs. Investigate any difference: wrong reference, reversed sign, absolute-vs-relative reference, wrong unit, division by the wrong row, or a rule from a comment that you missed.
+
+5. Preserve the source. Never edit or overwrite the source file; write to the requested destination path. Do not change original inputs, labels, units, headers or intentional blanks, do not insert or delete rows or columns, and keep number formats as they are unless the task explicitly asks for a format. Do not convert the whole sheet to percentages or add new label rows.
+
+6. Deliver exactly the requested path, then state briefly: the formulas you used per row, and the checked values. Do not claim correctness you did not verify with a recalculation.
